## Your rights at work

Type 2 diabetes can count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life, judged as if you were **not taking your treatment**. If it counts, your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- **breaks** to eat, check your blood sugar or take medicine
- time off for **appointments**, eye screening and education courses
- **regular shift patterns**, if changing shifts makes diabetes hard to manage

## Free prescriptions

In **England**, if you take **medicine for diabetes** (not diet alone), you can get a **medical exemption certificate**, which gives you free prescriptions for all your medicines. Ask your GP surgery for the form. Prescriptions are **free for everyone in Wales**.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how your health affects your daily life. Type 2 diabetes on its own does not usually affect people enough to qualify. But **complications** can, for example:

- **nerve damage (neuropathy)** causing pain, numbness or poor balance, which can affect walking, washing and cooking safely
- **sight loss** from diabetic eye disease, which can affect reading, cooking and getting around
- **foot ulcers or amputation**, which affect moving around
- **kidney disease** or heart disease causing tiredness and breathlessness

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you are over State Pension age, look at [Attendance Allowance](/benefits/attendance-allowance/) instead. If you cannot work because of your health, you may be able to get **[Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/)** or **[New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/)**.
